@page "/form-validation"

<CatHead Title="Validation">
    A field is at rest, rejected or unusual. Rejected keys off <code>aria-invalid</code>, never a
    class, so the styling and what a screen reader announces cannot disagree; unusual is
    <code>data-state="warn"</code>. Both go on the control, every field family draws both, and
    <code>aria-describedby</code> on the control makes the message part of the field.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormValidation.Messages" Title="Error and warning" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-error</code> is a rejected value. <code>.form-warning</code> is one that is
        accepted but unwise &mdash; its field carries <code>data-state="warn"</code> and no
        <code>aria-invalid</code>, because the value is kept.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormValidation.Unusual" Title="Unusual, not rejected" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>data-state="warn"</code> is for a value that is saved but worth a second look &mdash;
        outside the usual range, far from the average. Red would tell the reader it was refused.
        No ARIA attribute says "unusual", so the <code>.form-warning</code> tied by
        <code>aria-describedby</code> is what a screen reader hears; write it every time. On an
        <code>.input-group</code> the attribute still goes on the control, and the group draws the
        edge.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormValidation.AcrossControls" Title="Across every control" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        One attribute, the same result on an input, a select, an <code>.input-group</code> and a
        text area &mdash; and on a combo box, a colour field and both editors. In a group, the
        attribute stays on the control and the group draws the edge. The select is the case
        worth knowing: it needs an empty first option for <code>required</code> to bite at all.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormValidation.Required" Title="Marking a field required" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-label--required</code> draws the marker and <code>required</code> on the control
        does the enforcing. Write both: the marker is decoration, and a screen reader is told by the
        attribute.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormValidation.InAGrid" Title="Inside a two-up row" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The message belongs to the field, so it sits under that field's control and not under the
        row; the <a href="/grid"><code>.sedna-grid</code></a> keeps the two columns aligned at the
        top. A form that rejected more than one field also says so once, at the top, in a
        <a href="/alert">validation summary</a> linking to each.
    </p>
</CatExample>
